{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01MpvTk1xD25kAWkmqfJMqZZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q6 — Essential components of an agent beyond an LLM?**\n\n---\n\n**Answer note:** `ai-engineering/answers/03-006-essential-components-of-an-agent-beyond-an-llm.md`\n\nThe note covers a **four-component architecture** that every production agent requires beyond the LLM:\n\n1. **Tool Layer** — JSON-schemed actuators (the orchestrator executes them, not the LLM). Emphasises that the `description` field in the schema is the LLM's interface to the tool — schema quality directly determines tool-selection accuracy.\n\n2. **Memory Layer** — four types:\n   - Working (context window, bounded)\n   - Episodic (vector store — Pinecone/Qdrant, similarity retrieval)\n   - Semantic (KV store / structured DB — Redis/PostgreSQL, exact-match recall)\n   - Procedural (system prompt / fine-tune, learned behaviors)\n\n3. **Orchestrator / Planner** — the control-flow engine: drives the loop, enforces turn/token budgets, handles retries with idempotency, triggers HITL on irreversible actions, checkpoints state (LangGraph StateGraph, Temporal).\n\n4. **Observability & Safety** — LangSmith/Arize traces, NeMo Guardrails / LlamaGuard, Presidio PII scrubbing, action allowlist/denylist as the primary prompt-injection defense.\n\nIncludes a concrete LangGraph support-agent stack and a failure-mode table (what breaks when each component is missing).\n\n**33 answer notes remaining** in category 03-agents-tool-use (Q7–Q38 + validate)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":363,"cache_read_input_tokens":65365,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":363,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c6a5410-8272-4608-870e-772871015af8","uuid":"